This is a fundamental principle of the adversarial system and the right against self-incrimination. While the court can compel a witness to attend and answer questions, they cannot force a non-party to provide testimony that might incriminate them or their own interests, nor can they force a party to testify against themselves.

Therefore, the legal rule is: **A person who is not a party to the proceedings cannot be compelled to give evidence.**
